# PREPARE checks, in order.
> PREPARE p (INT) AS SELECT $1
!> PREPARE p (INT) AS SELECT $1
!> PREPARE p AS BEGIN
!> PREPARE q AS BEGIN
!> PREPARE q (INT) AS SELECT $1, $3, $2
!> PREPARE q AS SELECT * FROM t WHERE id = $1
> PREPARE q (INT, INT, INT) AS SELECT $3
!> EXECUTE p ($1)
---
Error: invalid input: prepared statement p already exists
Error: invalid input: prepared statement p already exists
Error: invalid input: PREPARE is not supported for BEGIN
Error: invalid input: no type given for parameter $2
Error: invalid input: no type given for parameter $1
Error: invalid input: parameter $1 can only be used in a prepared statement

# PREPARE doesn't check the statement against the database; EXECUTE does.
> PREPARE bad AS SELECT nosuch FROM nowhere
!> EXECUTE bad
> DEALLOCATE bad
!> DEALLOCATE bad
---
Error: invalid input: table nowhere does not exist
Error: invalid input: prepared statement bad does not exist

